  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA broad upper trough will be setting up across the eastern U.S.\nthis weekend with an upper low dropping southeastward out of \nCanada and becoming a closed low over the Great Lakes on Sunday.\nA surface low is expected to develop over the eastern Great \nLakes region as well tonight into Sunday. These features are \nexpected to be the main drivers of our weather into early next \nweek.\n\nConditions have remained relatively stable this afternoon. This,\ncombined with the lack of a forcing mechanism has allowed for \nthe area to remain rain-free for now. Can't rule out an isolated\nshower later this afternoon, but otherwise most of the rain\nshowers and isolated thunderstorms will hold off until tonight.\n\nThe surface low over the eastern Great Lakes is expected to \nlift north-northeast, passing just north and west of the area \nSunday. As it shifts north toward the Ontario/Quebec border, it \nwill drag a surface cold front across the region Sunday. Latest\ntrends have this cold front moving through in the morning, which\nkeeps the greatest chance of showers and thunderstorms during\nthat timeframe. As the winds shift to more westerly following\nthe passage of the front, isolated to scattered showers and\nthunderstorms may develop Sunday afternoon (greatest chance in\nCentral NY), but coverage of these should be rather limited.\n\nBy Monday, the 500mb closed low moves overhead. This combined \nwith with cooler air aloft moving over the Great Lakes with \n850mb temps falling to +6C to +10C and a west to northwest flow\nin place, additional lake effect/lake enhanced rain showers are\nexpected to develop. As the upper low lifts NE Tuesday, there \ncould be a few lingering lake effect rain showers downwind of \nLake Ontario near and north of the NYS Thruway before drier \nconditions return.\n\nNear to slightly below normal temperatures are expected this \nweekend before turning noticeably cooler Monday with the upper \nlow overhead with highs in the 60s and lower 70s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure briefly builds in Tuesday night into Wednesday \nwith dry conditions. Then Thursday into Friday, a similar \npattern to this weekend will likely set back up with a \npotential low moving up from the south and another upper trough \npushing in from the west that would bring the chance for \nadditional showers and thunderstorms. As of now these features \nappear to be more progressive and look to move out of the region\nby next weekend.",
